Scenario-Based DevOps Interview Question

1. Question:
 Your DevOps team uses Terraform to manage AWS infrastructure. Multiple engineers sometimes run terraform apply at the same time, which can corrupt the Terraform state file.
 How would you prevent state corruption and ensure only one person modifies the state at a time?

Answer (Production-Oriented):

To prevent Terraform state corruption when multiple engineers work on the same infrastructure, we should store the state in a remote backend and enable state locking.

In newer Terraform versions, when using the S3 backend, we can enable native state locking using use_lockfile = true. This creates a lock file (.tflock) in the S3 bucket whenever someone runs terraform apply.

If another team member tries to run Terraform at the same time, Terraform detects the lock file and blocks the operation until the first process completes.

This ensures only one person modifies the infrastructure state at a time, preventing state corruption.

Example Terraform Backend Configuration

terraform {
 backend "s3" {
 bucket = "devops-terraform-state"
 key = "prod/terraform.tfstate"
 region = "us-east-1"
 encrypt = true
 use_lockfile = true
 }
}

How It Works in Practice

Engineer A runs: - terraform apply

Terraform creates a lock file in S3: - terraform.tfstate.tflock

If Engineer B tries to run: - terraform apply

Terraform will return an error similar to: - Error acquiring the state lock

Engineer B must wait until Engineer A finishes and the lock file is removed.

When Engineer A finishes the Terraform operation, the lock file is automatically removed by Terraform.

